WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:15.520
I'm Ed Bertschinger, MIT'S
Institute, Community,

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:17.470
and Equity Officer.

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:20.430
It's my privilege to welcome
you to this community dialogue

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:22.760
responding to recent tragedies.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:27.990
To introduce the event and to
prepare you for the work ahead,

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:32.200
I begin with a poem by
Juan Felipe Herrera,

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:35.780
Poet Laureate of
the United States,

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:43.450
called "@ the Crossroads,
a Sudden American Poem."

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:50.040
"RIP Philando Castile, Alton
Sterling, Dallas police

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:56.460
officers, Lorne Ahrens,
Michael Krol, Michael J. Smith,

00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:00.690
Brent Thompson, and
Patrick Zamarippa,

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:02.810
and all their families.

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:06.800
And to all those injured.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:11.730
Let us celebrate the lives
of all as we reflect and pray

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:14.300
and meditate on
their brutal deaths.

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:17.400
Let us celebrate those
who marched at night

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:23.810
and who spoke of peace and
chanted Black Lives Matter.

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:27.960
Let us celebrate the
officers dressed in blues,

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:31.040
ready to protect.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:34.310
Let us know the departed
as we did not know them

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:41.830
before, their faces, bodies,
names, what they loved,

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:46.550
their words, the stories
they often spoke.

00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:50.090
"Before we return to the
usual business of our days,

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:53.520
let us know their
lives intimately.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:56.960
Let us take this moment
and, as impossible

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:02.300
as this may sound, let us
find the beauty in their lives

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:08.259
in the midst of their sudden
and never imagined vanishing.

00:02:08.259 --> 00:02:10.870
"Let us consider the
Dallas shooter-- what

00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:14.540
made him, what happened
in Afghanistan,

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:19.010
what flames burned inside.

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:22.660
Who was that man in Baton Rouge
with the red shirt selling

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:24.850
CDs in the parking lot?

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:26.950
Who was that man in
Minnesota toppled

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:30.610
on the car seat with
a perforated arm

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:34.470
and a continent-shaped flood
of blood on his white T?

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:40.590
Who was that man, prone and
gone by the night pillar of El

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:44.620
Centro college in Dallas?

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:49.670
This could be the first
step in the new evaluation

00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:50.730
of our society.

00:02:50.730 --> 00:03:02.150
This could be the first
step of all of our lives."

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:04.450
This is why we are here.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:07.480
We were invited
by President Reif,

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:09.790
who sends his regrets that
he cannot be president

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:12.600
because of international travel.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:16.020
But many of MIT's
senior leaders are here,

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:21.280
including Provost Marty Schmidt
and Chancellor Cindy Barnhart.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:24.960
We are here in answer to
President Reif's call, when

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:28.750
he wrote, "I believe our
leading civic institutions have

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:30.680
a responsibility
to speak clearly

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:34.640
against these corrosive
forces, and to act practically

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:37.540
to inspire and create
positive change.

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:40.040
In this time of need,
the MIT community

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:42.550
has an opportunity to offer
service of great value

00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:46.770
to our society, to our country,
and perhaps to the world

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:51.820
by applying our unique strengths
to the problem at hand.

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:56.610
But we are also here because of
our individual cries of pain,

00:03:56.610 --> 00:04:02.460
pangs of fear, and desire
for peace and healing.

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:06.730
MIT prides itself on
its mind and hand.

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:10.480
Today, we are
exercising our heart.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:13.900
"Our goal today is not
to solve a problem.

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:16.610
This event is not
about solutions.

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:20.529
We can't solve a problem we
can't hear each other talking

00:04:20.529 --> 00:04:22.620
about.

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:25.840
We can't solve a problem we
can't hear each other talking

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:27.200
about.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:30.670
First, we must listen to each
other with our whole hearts,

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:35.490
to feel, to cry, to
be held together.

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:38.370
"You will be
uncomfortable today.

00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:42.200
If you're not uncomfortable,
we're not growing.

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:44.220
Stay with us.

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:47.570
Take strength from our students
who shared their vulnerability

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:50.310
in the MIT admissions blogs.

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:54.230
Read them, and share them
with your friends and family.

00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:58.090
"Take strength in knowing that
everyone here-- faculty, summer

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:03.530
students, alumni, MIT police,
support staff-- everyone

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:07.840
is here to be together
in a caring community.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:11.510
We care deeply, so deeply
that it sometimes hurts.

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:14.100
That is OK.

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:15.130
Pay attention now.

00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:17.720
Here is the program.

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:23.540
After the next speaker, you will
focus on your table as a group.

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:25.500
Each table has a
facilitator who is

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.810
prepared to help work
with everyone to address

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:30.160
some questions of the heart.

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:32.890
Your facilitator will present
discussion ground rules

00:05:32.890 --> 00:05:35.240
we invite you to respect.

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:39.150
You'll have until 1:20
PM to explore together.

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:41.550
There's no question and
answer to the whole group.

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:44.320
We're in four different rooms.

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:46.970
You will have a chance to
send notes and recommendations

00:05:46.970 --> 00:05:48.450
to the organizers
by writing them

00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:50.570
on index cards at each table.

00:05:50.570 --> 00:05:53.080
We will collect these and share
a summary with the community

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:55.491
by early fall.

00:05:55.491 --> 00:05:57.990
I want to give thanks to those
who made this event possible,

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:01.160
starting with President
Reif, whose passionate call

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:03.220
for a response to the
killings last week

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:05.180
has brought us together.

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:09.230
Thank you to Ted Johnson and his
big team at Institute Events,

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:11.940
and MIT Video Productions
for pulling together

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:14.160
a major event in one week.

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:16.440
Thank you to the working
group on support staff

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:18.040
issues for their volunteers.

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:21.270
And thank you to the many
facilitators serving today.

00:06:21.270 --> 00:06:23.710
Thank you to Kirk
Kolenbrander, to Alice Johnson,

00:06:23.710 --> 00:06:26.950
Abigail Francis, and La-Tarri
Canty, Kester Barrow,

00:06:26.950 --> 00:06:29.930
[? Dianetta ?] [? Creighton. ?]
Please join me in thanking them

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:30.730
all.

00:06:30.730 --> 00:06:33.610
[APPLAUSE]

00:06:40.350 --> 00:06:43.440
After this event, we invite
you to a post-event time

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:46.600
for prayer and reflection
with Chaplain Andrew Innocent

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:50.940
in Room 491 upstairs, where you
can decompress and contribute

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:53.400
if you wish to an
art project called

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:54.920
"Thoughts from the Heart."

00:06:54.920 --> 00:06:58.090
Now, it is my pleasure to
introduce MIT Area Director

00:06:58.090 --> 00:07:00.750
Kester Barrow of the
Division of Student Life who

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:02.900
will offer some important
framing of today's

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:04.530
conversations.

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:05.340
Mr. Barrow.

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:08.390
[APPLAUSE]

